WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:10.407
Look, up in the sky!

2
00:00:11.248 --> 00:00:11.889
Is it a bird?

3
00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:13.410
Is it a plane?

4
00:00:14.291 --> 00:00:15.752
It's a red-tailed hawk!

5
00:00:16.612 --> 00:00:22.677
They call him Pale Male because he has a large patch of white feathers on his chest, and

6
00:00:23.018 --> 00:00:23.738
he is male.

7
00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:28.022
He came out of nowhere to try to make it in the big city.

8
00:00:28.402 --> 00:00:30.604
He is brave and fearless.

9
00:00:32.244 --> 00:00:37.548
There are many birds that live in New York City's Central Park.

10
00:00:37.729 --> 00:00:43.393
In 1990, 215 different species lived there—but no hawks.

11
00:00:44.514 --> 00:00:49.438
Then, in the fall of 1991, Pale Male appeared.

12
00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:55.503
He was the first hawk to make his home in Central Park, the most visited city park in

13
00:00:55.543 --> 00:00:56.544
the United States.

14
00:00:57.256 --> 00:01:03.020
Hawks usually live in the open countryside, so people were very excited to see him.

15
00:01:03.981 --> 00:01:10.085
They enjoyed watching him hunt for rats, pigeons, and squirrels from high atop his perch.

16
00:01:11.005 --> 00:01:18.891
Red-tailed hawks like to watch for prey—the creatures that other animals kill and eat—from the tops of tall

17
00:01:18.991 --> 00:01:26.456
trees. But since Pale Male lived in the city, his favorite perches were the tops of apartment buildings!

18
00:01:27.308 --> 00:01:32.831
Fifth Avenue, which lines Central Park, is where many rich and famous people live.

19
00:01:33.992 --> 00:01:36.053
Pale Male sat on their balconies.

20
00:01:37.154 --> 00:01:41.917
When he saw a rat or pigeon on the ground, he would swoop down to catch it.

21
00:01:42.657 --> 00:01:46.360
People were amazed that a hawk could survive this way in the city.

22
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.584
People were even more amazed when, the next spring, Pale Male brought a mate to New York.

23
00:01:54.356 --> 00:01:58.137
Together they built a nest in a tall tree in the park.

24
00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:03.959
But crows, a natural enemy of red-tailed hawks, drove the pair away.

25
00:02:04.699 --> 00:02:05.539
What would they do?

26
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.880
Pale Male had an idea.

27
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:16.042
He and his mate broke twigs from a tree and flew to the top of a 12-story apartment

28
00:02:16.082 --> 00:02:20.963
building. They built their nest on a ledge above someone's apartment window!

29
00:02:21.864 --> 00:02:23.324
They chose a good spot.

30
00:02:23.872 --> 00:02:27.615
The spikes on the ledge helped keep the nest firmly in place.

31
00:02:28.375 --> 00:02:31.157
And the ledge gave them a great view of the park.

32
00:02:32.138 --> 00:02:34.019
Soon the family grew.

33
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:39.443
When three chicks were seen in the nest, people watching below cheered.

34
00:02:40.144 --> 00:02:43.146
They had been waiting a long time to see the chicks.

35
00:02:43.906 --> 00:02:47.749
Pale Male and his mate fed the baby chicks every day.

36
00:02:48.429 --> 00:02:51.772
When the babies were ready, they learned to fly from their parents.

37
00:02:52.428 --> 00:02:55.389
Pale Male and his mate were good parents.

38
00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.971
Pale Male was becoming more and more famous.

39
00:02:59.791 --> 00:03:03.613
A devoted group of bird-watchers followed his every move.

40
00:03:04.433 --> 00:03:10.856
New Yorkers grew to love him as they read about his adventures in the newspapers and saw pictures

41
00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:13.017
of him and his family on the Internet.

42
00:03:13.877 --> 00:03:16.938
He became a part of everyday life in the city.

43
00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:20.120
But not everyone loved the hawks.

44
00:03:21.096 --> 00:03:26.200
Red-tailed hawks are large birds, and they keep adding twigs to their nests.

45
00:03:27.001 --> 00:03:29.944
The apartment people didn't like the mess they made.

46
00:03:30.744 --> 00:03:36.469
Pale Male's nest was now eight feet wide and weighed four hundred pounds!

47
00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:42.354
People complained that bits of the nest and pigeon parts kept falling to the ground.

48
00:03:42.774 --> 00:03:44.736
They wanted to get rid of the nest.

49
00:03:45.797 --> 00:03:53.177
One cold and rainy day in December 2004, the apartment people got permission to take down the nest.

50
00:03:54.458 --> 00:03:56.439
Pale Male's home was gone.

51
00:03:57.579 --> 00:04:00.020
The next day everyone knew what had happened.

52
00:04:01.201 --> 00:04:04.982
It was in the newspapers and on TV all over the world.

53
00:04:06.063 --> 00:04:08.764
Soon people gathered at the building to protest.

54
00:04:09.944 --> 00:04:13.666
They carried signs: HONK 4 HAWKS!

55
00:04:14.306 --> 00:04:17.067
NYC KIDS FOR PALE MALE!

56
00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:18.968
BRING BACK THE NEST!

57
00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:26.530
Cars, buses, cabdrivers, even police in their cruisers honked in support as they drove by.

58
00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.871
People who loved Pale Male wrote letters to the apartment people.

59
00:04:31.891 --> 00:04:35.732
It took about three weeks, but finally the two groups met.

60
00:04:36.773 --> 00:04:38.153
The hawk lovers won.

61
00:04:39.073 --> 00:04:41.654
Pale Male could now build a new nest.

62
00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:49.056
Experts were called in to design a better support structure for it, one that would make everyone happy.

63
00:04:50.612 --> 00:04:53.753
And the happiest of all was—Pale Male, of course!

64
00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:59.635
As of 2014 he is still living on the ledge in New York City.

65
00:05:00.936 --> 00:05:03.817
He has raised many chicks with all of his mates.

66
00:05:05.478 --> 00:05:08.339
There are even books and movies about Pale Male.

67
00:05:08.939 --> 00:05:11.480
Now that's a pretty amazing story!
